WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.850
In the world of communication, we have
two dominant approaches. Monologue and

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.200
dialogue. And they both have implications for how we come across as leaders. So

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.480
let's discuss these and unpack the
details.

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:19.460
Hello again, friends. Alex Lyon here.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.869
This channel, Communication Coach,
is here for rising leaders like you.

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:28.769
All the videos here are to help you increase your personal impact so you can lead the

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:32.579
people around you to higher levels of
excellence. And so if you haven't yet

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:36.899
subscribed, I encourage you to do so. Now a big part of leadership is your

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:42.690
communication style. Are you more likely
to monologue with people or dialogue.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:47.670
A monologue is where you communicate in a one-way fashion, where you mostly talk

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:52.260
at other people, not with other people.
When you're monologuing, there's not a lot

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.370
of invitation to exchange ideas or for
them to take a turn and check for

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:00.210
understanding. Typically the goal of a
monologue whether you realize it or not

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:05.939
is you're trying to get your idea into
the other person's head. So that's one

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:10.439
approach now generally speaking people
don't really like to be on the receiving

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:14.850
end of a monologue. It tends to push
people away. The next time they see you

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.320
coming it's very likely that they'll try
to avoid you at least I probably

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.670
would. So second, let's look at the
dialogue approach to communication.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:29.009
A dialogue is by definition two ways.
There's lots of turn-taking lots of

00:01:29.009 --> 00:01:32.520
invitation going back and forth in the
conversation both verbally and

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:38.130
non-verbally. The goal of this is not to
implant your idea into somebody else's

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:44.460
head, but instead to reach a mutual
understanding or a shared meaning. Now

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.149
that does not mean that you agree with
what the other person is saying and

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.780
they've convinced you. You just
understand where they're coming from and

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:56.040
you can appreciate their point of view
in an accurate sense. So at least you do

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.450
clearly follow what they're saying. Now
somewhere along the line you're probably

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:05.520
going to run into a clever salesperson
or somebody who really has some

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.959
experience. And they will be monologuing at you but they're still doing this

00:02:09.959 --> 00:02:13.409
thing where they're taking turns.
It seems like there's some

00:02:13.409 --> 00:02:17.430
invitation. You're having a conversation
but in the end they're steering you

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:22.860
toward their desired outcome. I've had
a lot of skilled salespeople drive me

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.920
toward their way of thinking. So they're
still trying to get their idea into my

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.930
head and that's still a monologue, what
Martin Buber calls a "Monologue disguised

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.879
as a dialogue." So when I ask you do are
you more likely to monologue or a

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:40.290
dialogue, I'm talking about a dialogue in
the genuine sense where you actually

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.250
desire a mutual exchange of ideas. So,
question of the day.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.569
Are you a "monologue-er" or a "dialogue-er"? I hope I'm saying that right. I would

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:52.890
love to hear your questions/comments in that section below. So, thanks. God bless. And I

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.609
will see you next time.

